The debug_assert_eq!(val_ty(imm), from_backend_ty) holds trivially
for LLVM (opaque pointers, uniform scalar types) but is wrong for
nominally-typed backends: cg_gcc carries i8* where the canonical
pointer is void*, and size_t where the scalar says __int64_t — same
layout, different gccjit type objects. The match right below
bitcasts/pointercasts per primitive kind, which is the meaningful
validation; a truly mismatched immediate still fails inside the cast.

Unblocks the importer-binding new()/Box paths under
-Zcodegen-backend=gcc: the full subclass_cpp_base demo (pure +
concrete overrides, new Rust virtual, virtual dtor via deleting
thunk) passes on the pure-GCC pipeline; LLVM backend re-verified on
the same probes.
---
 compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/rvalue.rs | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/rvalue.rs b/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/rvalue.rs
index 2cb96c4ec0f..ae675ffbc2f 100644
--- a/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/rvalue.rs
+++ b/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/rvalue.rs
@@ -965,7 +965,14 @@ pub(super) fn transmute_scalar<'a, 'tcx, Bx: BuilderMethods<'a, 'tcx>>(
     imm = bx.from_immediate(imm);
 
     let from_backend_ty = bx.cx().type_from_scalar(from_scalar);
-    debug_assert_eq!(bx.cx().val_ty(imm), from_backend_ty);
+    // P09.x (v1.15): no strict `val_ty(imm) == from_backend_ty`
+    // pre-check here (upstream has a `debug_assert_eq`). Nominally
+    // typed backends (cg_gcc) legitimately carry immediates whose
+    // backend type differs from the canonical scalar type while
+    // being layout-identical — `i8*` vs `void*`, `size_t` vs
+    // `__int64_t`. The match below bitcasts / pointercasts per
+    // primitive kind, which is the meaningful validation; a truly
+    // mismatched immediate still fails inside the cast.
     let to_backend_ty = bx.cx().type_from_scalar(to_scalar);
 
     // If we have a scalar, we must already know its range. Either
